ommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (disease)\nAssess disease risk (red rot, smut, rust, leaf spot) from humidity, temperature, and the symptoms the farmer describes. Advise isolation/removal and when to consult a plant pathologist.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 49542 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary:\nIron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane, especially on calcareous soils, and worsens in successive ratoon crops. IISR documents six treatment options for chlorosis management and identifies the broader set of micronutrients (B, Cu, Fe, S, Mg, Mn, Mo, Zn) affecting cane growth, along with critical soil deficiency thresholds.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations:\n- Farmers on calcareous or high-pH soils (common in parts of Karnataka's black-soil tracts) should watch for chlorosis symptoms (interveinal yellowing of younger leaves) especially in ratoon crops, and apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application, since the source material documents six distinct combined strategies rather than one standalone fix.\n- Soil testing against the documented critical thresholds (e.g., Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type) should guide whether a general basal micronutrient mix or a targeted single-nutrient correction is warranted.\n- During drought-related contingency management, proactively apply the FeSO₄+ZnSO₄+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for visible chlorosis symptoms, since iron deficiency is a known secondary effect of extended dry spells.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information:\n- Iron chlorosis has been observed in nearly all sugarcane-growing states of India, particularly Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, and Bihar; it is most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.\n- **Six documented treatments for lime-induced iron chlorosis:**\n  1. Foliar spray of 2% FeSO₄ solution combined with 0.5% MnSO₄ and 2% urea, applied two to three times.\n  2. Soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg/ha.\n  3. Soil application of press mud (5 t/ha) combined with iron pyrite (2 t/ha).\n  4. Soil application of farm yard manure (25 t/ha) plus foliar application of 1.5% FeSO₄ with 1% urea at weekly intervals, and 1% ZnSO₄ at monthly intervals.\n  5. Soil application of elemental sulphur at 30–40 kg/ha, applied 3 weeks before planting.\n  6. Foliar spray of Fe-EDTA (50 ppm solution).\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - ಸಮಯ: 2026‑07‑26 16:40 UTC (node demo‑node‑1) - ತಾಪಮಾನ: 23.0 °C (ಬದಲಾವಣೆ ಇಲ್ಲ) - ಆರ್ದ್ರತೆ: 64.2 % (ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ ಇಳಿದಿದೆ) - ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ತೇವಾಂಶ: 0.0 % (ವಾಲ್ಯೂಮೆಟ್ರಿಕ್) – ಹಿಂದಿನ ಮಾಪನದಿಂದ 49 % ಕುಸಿತ (49 % → 0 %) - ಸಕ್ರಿಯ ಎಚ್ಚರಿಕೆ: **Low ...\nFarmer: ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - Vision: ಚಿತ್ರವನ್ನು ಬ್ರೌನ್ ಸ್ಪಾಟ್ (brown spot) ಎಂದು 95% ವಿಶ್ವಾಸದೊಂದಿಗೆ ವರ್ಗೀಕರಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ. - Temperature: 25.0 °C - Humidity: 55.0 % - Soil moisture: 40.0 % - Active alerts: - [warning] High humidity 88 % (ರೋಗದ ಅಪಾಯ) - ...\nFarmer: Nodi anna, sugarcane borer training IISR yavaga nadesidru confirm madi.\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - ICAR‑IISR ಜ್ಞಾನದಲ್ಲಿ “Identification and Management of Major Sugarcane Borers” ಎಂಬ ಒಂದಿನ ದಿನದ ರೈತ ತರಬೇತಿ ಕಾರ್ಯಕ್ರಮವನ್ನು ICAR‑IISR Biological Control Centre, Pravaranagar (Maharashtra) ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಡೆಸಲಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ಉಲ್ಲೇಖಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ. ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nRatoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "farmer": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli."}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "sarvam": "Cause of chlorosis in rotted crop and how to fix it.", "groq": "Ratoon sugarcane chlorosis causes and management strategies"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8366, "sarvam": 0.877, "groq": 0.8871}, "retrieved": [{"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary", "score": 0.8871, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8787, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information", "score": 0.877, "via": "sarvam"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation**  \n- “Iron chlorosis has been observed … it is most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.”  \n- “Six documented treatments for lime‑induced iron chlorosis” are listed, including foliar spray of 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea (2‑3 times) and soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg / ha.  \n- Soil testing thresholds: Fe < 4.2–6.3 ppm (depending on soil type) indicate deficiency.  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- In ratoon crops the same soil is reused; lime (calcium carbonate) in calcareous soils raises pH, which reduces iron solubility and makes Fe less available to the plant.  \n- Repeated ratooning depletes the limited available iron further, so chlorosis becomes more pronounced.  \n- Drought periods can aggravate the problem by limiting Fe uptake, hence proactive iron nutrition is advised.  \n\n**Confidence**  \nHigh – the explanation and management options are directly stated in the provided agricultural knowledge.  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. Conduct a soil test for Fe; if Fe < 4.2 ppm (or < 6.3 ppm on the relevant soil type) proceed with a combined iron correction.  \n2. Apply one of the documented combined strategies, e.g.:  \n   - Foliar spray of 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea, repeat 2‑3 times at 7‑day intervals.  \n   - Simultaneously apply soil FeSO₄ at 25 kg / ha (or incorporate press‑mud 5 t / ha + iron pyrite 2 t / ha).  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Inspect younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing (early chlorosis sign).  \n- Re‑measure soil Fe after the first soil application (if feasible) to confirm increase toward the 4.2–6.3 ppm range.  \n- Keep a record of foliar spray dates and visual response; repeat foliar sprays if yellowing persists after 10 days.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If chlorosis does not improve after two foliar applications and one soil FeSO₄ dose.  \n- If soil test shows Fe still below the critical threshold despite applications.  \n- If other nutrient deficiency symptoms (e.g., stunted growth, necrosis) appear, indicating possible multi‑micronutrient imbalance.", "claims": ["supported: Iron chlorosis is most severe on calcareous soils and intensifies with successive ratoons", "supported: Six documented treatments for lime‑induced iron chlorosis exist", "supported: Foliar spray of 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea applied 2‑3 times is a documented treatment", "supported: Soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg / ha is a documented treatment", "supported: Fe soil testing threshold is Fe < 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type", "supported: If Fe < 4.2 ppm (or < 6.3 ppm on the relevant soil type) proceed with a combined iron correction", "supported: Foliar spray of 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea should be repeated 2‑3 times at 7‑day intervals", "supported: Simultaneously apply soil FeSO₄ at 25 kg / ha or incorporate press‑mud 5 t / ha + iron pyrite 2 t / ha", "supported: Inspect younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing as early chlorosis sign", "supported: Re‑measure soil Fe after the first soil application to confirm increase toward 4.2–6.3 ppm range", "supported: Repeat foliar sprays if yellowing persists after 10 days", "supported: Seek expert help if chlorosis does not improve after two foliar applications and one soil FeSO₄ dose", "supported: Seek expert help if soil test shows Fe still below the critical threshold despite applications", "supported: Seek expert help if other nutrient deficiency symptoms appear, indicating possible multi‑micronutrient imbalance"], "unsupported": [], "final_answer": "**Observation**  \n- “Iron chlorosis has been observed … it is most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.”  \n- “Six documented treatments for lime‑induced iron chlorosis” are listed, including foliar spray of 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea (2‑3 times) and soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg / ha.  \n- Soil testing thresholds: Fe < 4.2–6.3 ppm (depending on soil type) indicate deficiency.  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- In ratoon crops the same soil is reused; lime (calcium carbonate) in calcareous soils raises pH, which reduces iron solubility and makes Fe less available to the plant.  \n- Repeated ratooning depletes the limited available iron further, so chlorosis becomes more pronounced.  \n- Drought periods can aggravate the problem by limiting Fe uptake, hence proactive iron nutrition is advised.  \n\n**Confidence**  \nHigh – the explanation and management options are directly stated in the provided agricultural knowledge.  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. Conduct a soil test for Fe; if Fe < 4.2 ppm (or < 6.3 ppm on the relevant soil type) proceed with a combined iron correction.  \n2. Apply one of the documented combined strategies, e.g.:  \n   - Foliar spray of 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea, repeat 2‑3 times at 7‑day intervals.  \n   - Simultaneously apply soil FeSO₄ at 25 kg / ha (or incorporate press‑mud 5 t / ha + iron pyrite 2 t / ha).  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Inspect younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing (early chlorosis sign).  \n- Re‑measure soil Fe after the first soil application (if feasible) to confirm increase toward the 4.2–6.3 ppm range.  \n- Keep a record of foliar spray dates and visual response; repeat foliar sprays if yellowing persists after 10 days.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If chlorosis does not improve after two foliar applications and one soil FeSO₄ dose.  \n- If soil test shows Fe still below the critical threshold despite applications.  \n- If other nutrient deficiency symptoms (e.g., stunted growth, necrosis) appear, indicating possible multi‑micronutrient imbalance.", "fact_check": "checked", "fact_check_seconds": 1.19, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "rewrite", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 207, "completion_tokens": 38, "seconds": 0.53, "statu