ity consistent with the sensor readings and the symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (general)\nAnswer the farmer's question from the knowledge text, using this farm's data where it is relevant.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 49542 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Planting Methods and Advanced Nursery Techniques — Main Information:\n- **Tissue culture**: enables rapid multiplication of high-yielding, disease-resistant varieties; conventional vegetative propagation requires large seed quantities and has a low multiplication rate (10-12 years from variety release to field-scale coverage); tissue culture can compress this multiplication timeline to about 5 years and produces disease-free plantlets from apical meristem tissue.\n- **Spaced Transplanting (STP)**: raised nursery beds (3 ft wide) are prepared with FYM; single-bud setts are dipped in 0.2% Carbendazim, planted vertically, and treated with chlorpyriphos or imidacloprid against pests. Settlings are transplanted after 6–7 weeks, achieving 90–95% survival with careful management. Seed cane requirement drops to ~20 quintals/ha versus ~60 quintals/ha for normal planting.\n\nSugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Summary:\nSugarcane is vegetatively propagated using stem cuttings (\"setts\"), making seed quality, seed rate, and pre-planting treatment critical determinants of germination and final yield. IISR's package of practices documents optimum seed rates and spacing by state, along with seed treatment protocols to protect against soil-borne pests and diseases and to improve germination percentage.\n\nSugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Main Information:\n- Sugarcane requires a mean temperature of 28–32°C for optimal growth; temperatures above 45°C reduce tillering, and below 20°C slow growth. Areas with minimum temperature under 5°C are unsuitable.\n- The crop needs 75–120 cm of annual rainfall (if unirrigated) and a long growing season of 10–18 months. Ideal relative humidity is 70–85% during growth and 55–75% during ripening; below 50% humidity during growth is unsuitable.\n- India has two broad sugarcane regions:\n  - **Tropical region** (Maharashtra, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, Kerala): ~45% of area, 55% of national production; productivity ~80 t/ha.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: Prathi irrigation ge eshtu cm neeru hakbeku.\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"After irrigation, the soil‑moisture sensor should rise above the current 32.3 %\" (not in Gage's sources); \"Soil‑moisture readings remain low despite applying 7–8 cm water\" (not in Gage's sources). ..