s symptoms (interveinal yellowing of younger leaves) especially in ratoon crops, and apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application, since the source material documents six distinct combined strategies rather than one standalone fix.\n- Soil testing against the documented critical thresholds (e.g., Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type) should guide whether a general basal micronutrient mix or a targeted single-nutrient correction is warranted.\n- During drought-related contingency management, proactively apply the FeSO₄+ZnSO₄+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for visible chlorosis symptoms, since iron deficiency is a known secondary effect of extended dry spells.\n\nSugarcane Disease Management — Summary:\nSugarcane in India is affected by several economically damaging diseases — red rot, smut, wilt, grassy shoot disease, ratoon stunting disease, yellow leaf disease, pokkah boeng, and rust. IISR documents the causal organism and specific management approach for each, with an overarching emphasis on resistant varieties, sett/seed health, and hot-water/heat treatment as the primary defence.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.\nGage (earlier, shortened): ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"The reference text does not contain any information about specific sugarcane varieties or criteria for selecting the appropriate variety for Karnataka\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"I cannot ...\nFarmer: Sir every time watering how much depth I give, any standard number?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** The agricultural knowledge states: “Recommended irrigation depth per application: 7–8 cm.” **Analysis** Applying water to a depth of 7–8 cm each time will meet the standard irrigation amount recommended for sugarcane. ...\nFarmer: What is the FRP for sugarcane this season and how soon must the mill pay?\nGage (